package jan-klod:interfaces@0.3.0;

/// Drive a session from inside the sandbox — the inward twin of the client
/// protocol (Phase 22).
///
/// `jan-klod-protocol` is what a *client* says to the runtime: create a
/// session, list them, read one, fork it, send a message, answer a prompt.
/// This interface offers the same vocabulary *inward*, to a granted guest. The
/// symmetry is the point: a Telegram bot and a terminal client want the same
/// six things, and one of them happens to run in a component. Where the shapes
/// differ, `jan-klod-protocol` (`src/protocol/src/lib.rs`) is right and this
/// follows it.
///
/// It is the most powerful capability in the set — a holder drives the loop,
/// spends tokens, and reads what was said — so it is **default-deny**, granted
/// per component in `config.yaml`, declared in the manifest, and cross-checked
/// at boot against what the component actually imports, like every other grant.
///
/// Implemented by the host; imported by `chat-*` extensions and by anything
/// else that drives a session rather than participating in one.
///
/// # What a grant reaches
///
/// **The sessions the component created, plus the session that invoked it.**
/// `create-session` records the calling component as owner, and every later
/// call checks that record. A chat bot needs no configuration for this: it
/// creates a session per conversation, so it holds exactly its own.
///
/// A wider **prefix grant** exists for a component whose job is reading
/// sessions it did not create — an exporter, an archiver — and it is written in
/// `config.yaml` *and* declared in the manifest, like any other widening.
///
/// Lineage is **host-resolved**: a fork's parent is reachable because the host
/// knows the link, never because a guest named an id it guessed.
///
/// The surveyed harnesses land in the same place. Claude Code hands a hook one
/// `transcript_path` for *this* conversation; Codex passes the same triple, and
/// where it exposes a second session — a subagent's parent — the host resolves
/// it from the thread's own parent id rather than letting the extension ask.
/// oh-my-pi's `SessionManager.list(cwd)` is project-scoped, with "everything
/// everywhere" a separate `listAll()`. MCP, the one with a real boundary, gives
/// a server no conversation access at all.
///
/// One deliberate divergence, so it is not read as an oversight: those products
/// hand over a **filesystem path** and let OS permissions be the boundary —
/// nothing stops a Claude Code hook from opening a sibling session's JSONL. We
/// hand over a capability, which is narrower by construction. We copy their
/// *scope*, not their enforcement.
///
/// # When the driver dies mid-turn
///
/// A guest that traps while a turn runs leaves that turn **abandoned and
/// recorded incomplete**, through the conductor's existing path — not silently
/// dropped, and not left looking finished. A half-written answer that reads as
/// a whole one is the failure that path exists to prevent, and a driver
/// vanishing is the same situation as a client disconnecting mid-stream, which
/// the runtime already handles.
///
/// No function expresses this: it is host behaviour towards a guest that is no
/// longer there to be told. It is written here because the author of a driver
/// has to know what becomes of a turn they started and did not finish.
interface host-agent {
    use llm-types.{message};

    /// Opaque session identifier, the same string the client protocol uses.
    type session-id = string;

    /// Why a call failed.
    ///
    /// `busy` is its own case rather than a flavour of `failed` so a bot can
    /// tell "wait and retry" from "something is broken" and queue on its own
    /// side. Everything else the host might do about a busy session — a queue,
    /// a timeout, a priority — is scheduling policy, and the core holds
    /// mechanism, not policy.
    enum agent-error {
        /// No grant, or a session outside the one held. Indistinguishable on
        /// purpose: a guest learning that a session exists but is not its own
        /// has learned something a capability boundary should not tell it.
        denied,
        /// A turn is already running in this session. See `send-message`.
        busy,
        /// No such session, within what this component may reach.
        not-found,
        /// The host could not serve the call (store failure, boot state).
        failed,
    }

    /// One session as a lister sees it. Mirrors the client protocol's session
    /// list projection, which spells the same value `id`.
    record session-summary {
        id: session-id,
        /// First 80 characters of the first user message.
        preview: string,
    }

    /// A session's messages, as `session/get` returns them.
    record transcript {
        session: session-id,
        messages: list<message>,
        /// Log position of the last message, which `fork-session` takes as
        /// `at-seq`. Sparse: events with no message still consume a position.
        last-seq: u64,
    }

    /// What a fork produced.
    record fork-result {
        /// The child session. Generated by the host — a guest-picked id could
        /// collide with a live session.
        id: session-id,
        /// How many events were copied.
        copied: u64,
    }

    /// A tool the loop is about to run, or has run.
    record tool-event {
        /// Call id; a `tool-finished` carries the id of the `tool-started` it answers.
        id: string,
        /// Tool name on `tool-started`; empty on `tool-finished`.
        name: string,
        /// JSON arguments on `tool-started`, result content on `tool-finished`.
        body: string,
        /// Whether the call failed. Meaningful on `tool-finished` only.
        failed: bool,
    }

    /// A question the turn is blocked on. Answer it with `answer-prompt`;
    /// leaving it unanswered takes `default-answer`, as everywhere else.
    record prompt {
        question: string,
        /// Empty means free text; non-empty means choose one.
        options: list<string>,
        default-answer: string,
    }

    /// What happened in a session since the last poll.
    ///
    /// The same events the client protocol notifies, in the same order. A
    /// driver that renders these renders what a terminal client would.
    variant event {
        /// A chunk of assistant text.
        text-delta(string),
        /// A tool is about to run, the `tool-call` gate having allowed it.
        tool-started(tool-event),
        /// A tool returned.
        tool-finished(tool-event),
        /// A non-fatal notice — a provider fallback, a degraded path.
        warning(string),
        /// The turn finished; carries the authoritative answer.
        done(string),
        /// The turn is blocked on the user.
        asked(prompt),
        /// The turn failed.
        failed(string),
    }

    /// Allocate a session, owned by the calling component.
    ///
    /// Ownership is what a default grant reaches, so this is the call that
    /// creates reach rather than a call that consumes it.
    create-session: func() -> result<session-id, agent-error>;

    /// The sessions this component may reach.
    ///
    /// Its own, and the one that invoked it. A component with a prefix grant
    /// sees that prefix. Never everything.
    list-sessions: func() -> result<list<session-summary>, agent-error>;

    /// Every session in the store.
    ///
    /// A **separate function behind its own grant flag**, not a parameter on
    /// `list-sessions`. A flag on a call is easy to pass by accident and
    /// invisible in a manifest; a second function is granted or it is not, and
    /// the grant says which one the component asked for. oh-my-pi draws the
    /// same line between `list(cwd)` and `listAll()` as an API-design choice;
    /// here it is a security boundary, so it is drawn in the interface.
    list-all-sessions: func() -> result<list<session-summary>, agent-error>;

    /// Read one session's messages.
    get-session: func(session: session-id) -> result<transcript, agent-error>;

    /// Fork a session at `at-seq` into a fresh child, owned by this component.
    ///
    /// The parent must be reachable; the child is the component's own from the
    /// moment it exists. This is the only place a guest gains reach to lineage,
    /// and the host resolves it — the guest names a session it already holds.
    fork-session: func(session: session-id, at-seq: u64) -> result<fork-result, agent-error>;

    /// Start a turn. Returns as soon as the turn is accepted; its events
    /// arrive through `next-events`.
    ///
    /// **Run to completion, nesting refused.** A session already running a turn
    /// returns `busy`. The alternative — a host-side queue — puts scheduling
    /// policy in the core, and a guest that wants a queue can hold one: it
    /// knows its own conversation, and the host does not.
    send-message: func(session: session-id, text: string) -> result<_, agent-error>;

    /// Take up to `max` events from the session's queue, oldest first.
    ///
    /// **Polling, not push.** The host is synchronous and has no path into a
    /// guest; inventing one for this capability would be a larger change than
    /// the capability. The bound mirrors what `host-process` already does with
    /// command output: a call returns what fits and the caller comes back.
    ///
    /// An empty list means nothing has happened yet, which is not an error. A
    /// guest wanting lower latency polls more often; a guest that dies stops
    /// polling, which is also what happens to its turn — see below.
    next-events: func(session: session-id, max: u32) -> result<list<event>, agent-error>;

    /// Answer the prompt a `asked` event carried.
    ///
    /// This is what makes the holder a **driver** rather than an observer, and
    /// it is the piece `host-http` could never have carried: a chat bot has to
    /// be able to put the loop's question to a person and hand the answer back.
    ///
    /// Answering when nothing is pending is `not-found`, not a silent success —
    /// a driver that answers into the void should learn that it did.
    answer-prompt: func(session: session-id, answer: string) -> result<_, agent-error>;
}
